            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The boundary between the earthly realm and the divine realm, serving as a vast canvas that declares God's glory, creative power, and sovereign order.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Genesis 1:6-8\",\"Psalm 19:1\",\"Psalm 150:1\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A symbol of the veil or crystalline barrier separating human perception from the unmediated presence of God's throne.\",\"basis\":[\"Ezekiel 1:22-26\",\"Revelation 4:6\",\"Exodus 24:10\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The unseen spiritual realm where angelic and demonic forces operate in spiritual warfare.\",\"basis\":[\"Ephesians 2:2\",\"Ephesians 6:12\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"An allegorical representation of the Holy Scripture or the Church, which separates spiritual truths (waters above) from earthly or fleshly desires (waters below).\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:6-8\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While the physical or scientific nature of the firmament (Hebrew 'raqia') is heavily debated between literalists and those who read it as ancient Near Eastern phenomenological language, its symbolic and theological function is consistently recognized across traditions as the dividing line between God's heavenly throne and the earthly domain, as well as the supreme display of His handiwork in general revelation.\"}",
